// One declaration's failure does not silence the next declaration's analysis.
//
// Two gates used to stop the compile at the first error rather than at the
// failing declaration, and between them they made a whole class of rule
// enforced only in files that were otherwise clean. That is how 'imm' came to
// look unenforced when it had been checked since 2021.
//
// - modTypeCheck type checked every declaration's signature first and then no
//   body at all unless that pass was error-free. One malformed signature
//   suppressed every body diagnostic in the module.
// - fnDclTypeCheck ran blockFlow only while the global error count was zero, so
//   one error anywhere earlier, of any stage, turned off immutability checking,
//   ErrorMove and the lifetime checks for everything after it.
//
// Both gates now live in fnDclTypeCheck, one before the body and one before
// flow, and both compare the error count against the one that call entered
// with. So each is about the declaration being analyzed and not about whatever
// failed elsewhere, whichever walk arrived at it -- the module's, or a use
// demanding it from the middle of another body. This file is the assertion, and
// it is the one file in the corpus that deliberately mixes the stages. Each
// function below fails in a different way and none of them silences the next.
//
// One gate is deliberately still global and is not what this asserts: name
// resolution returns before type checking begins if it reported anything
// (conec.c). Type checking a tree with unresolved names is a different and much
// larger question.

// A failure in the signature phase. Its own body is skipped -- checking it
// against a parameter whose type never resolved would report again at every use
// and name nothing the author can act on -- so nothing else here is annotated
// for this function.
fn signatureFails(v i32, h &fn(n i32) i32 {n}) i32 {  //~ ErrorNotTyped:28 "Expected a type"
  h(v)
}

// A failure in the body phase, reported even though a signature already failed
fn bodyFailsTypeCheck() i32 {
  true                       //~ ErrorInvType:3 "Return expression type does not match return type on function"
                             //~^^ ErrorInvType:25 "This is the declared function's return type" follow-on
}

// A flow diagnostic, reported only because the gate is per function now
fn immStillChecked() i32 {
  imm n = 3
  n = 5                      //~ ErrorNoMut:3 "You do not have permission to modify lval"
  n
}

// ... and a second offending function is reported too, where before only the
// first function to reach flow analysis ever was
fn immCheckedAgain() i32 {
  imm m = 7
  m = 9                      //~ ErrorNoMut:3 "You do not have permission to modify lval"
  m
}
